WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
It's hard to pick a favorite Tarantino&nbsp;
flick, but the man himself has finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.120
settled on his own number one choice, and it may&nbsp;
surprise some of the director's biggest fans.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.360
With three decades of filmmaking under his belt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.260
Quentin Tarantino is one of the&nbsp;
foremost auteurs of his generation.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:21.600
His filmography is full of rarely disputed&nbsp;
classics like "Pulp Fiction" and "Inglourious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.100
Basterds," and his material is known as&nbsp;
much for its genre-bending as it is for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:31.020
his penchant for hyperviolence and, of course, his&nbsp;
predilection for close-up shots of people's feet.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:36.120
His films have earned Academy Awards and&nbsp;
drawn reactionary backlash in equal measure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.160
but what's undeniable is that each one is&nbsp;
the product of a singular vision. A Tarantino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.240
movie stands out in a lineup because they're&nbsp;
movies no one else could have possibly made.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:51.000
Tarantino has always been vocal about his&nbsp;
opinions. Whether he's deriding the MCU or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.680
ranking "Star Trek" over "Star Wars", he's&nbsp;
not afraid of rocking the boat. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.300
when it comes to his own work, he's been reluctant&nbsp;
to pick a favorite movie from his catalog.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.380
Lately, though, Tarantino has stepped&nbsp;
away from the camera and turned to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:09.060
page. He published a novelization of&nbsp;
his last film, 2019's "Once Upon a Time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.780
in Hollywood," and now he's on a press&nbsp;
tour promoting his nonfiction volume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.700
"Cinema Speculation." Recently, Tarantino&nbsp;
visited "The Howard Stern Show" for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.230
wide-ranging interview during which he finally&nbsp;
named which of his films he likes best.

00:01:22.316 --> 00:01:26.036
"I really do think 'Once Upon a Time&nbsp;
in Hollywood' is my best movie."

00:01:26.036 --> 00:01:31.740
Starring Leonardo DiCaprio as actor Rick Dalton&nbsp;
and Brad Pitt as his stunt double Cliff Booth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:38.100
"Once Upon a Time in Hollywood" opened in 2019&nbsp;
to mostly positive critical reception. Set during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.960
the heyday of Hollywood during the late 1960s,&nbsp;
when Westerns were in vogue and Bruce Lee was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.760
in his prime, the movie has been described as&nbsp;
an ode to a bygone era of the film industry.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.180
However, the real flourish on&nbsp;
top of the proceedings comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.200
with Tarantino's decision to center the&nbsp;
events of the film around the infamous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.800
Tate-LaBianca murders committed&nbsp;
by Charles Manson's followers.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.260
Margot Robbie takes on the role of Sharon Tate,&nbsp;
the young starlet murdered on that fateful day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:10.800
in 1969. But in line with the director's previous&nbsp;
historical revisionisms, the movie climaxes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.300
Dalton and Booth, who never existed in&nbsp;
real life, inadvertently saving the day.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.620
Tarantino holds back on his usual&nbsp;
tendencies toward graphic violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:24.420
until that linchpin scene, then lets&nbsp;
it erupt in truly stunning fashion as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:28.800
the Manson Family members are taken out&nbsp;
with a dog, fists, and a flamethrower.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.140
"I love that stuff. All the killing."

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.700
"A lot of killing. Lot of killing."

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:37.740
Overall, it's a weird and wild movie that doesn't&nbsp;
seem to care about audience sensibilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.880
and it's branded with Tarantino's&nbsp;
signature stylistic flourishes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:45.960
front to back. It's easy to see why the&nbsp;
director would think it's his best work.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.340
It's clear that Tarantino was given&nbsp;
total leeway. The plot meanders through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.720
deviations and digressions, cycles&nbsp;
back on itself recursively, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.860
because this is a Tarantino film, the&nbsp;
camera fixates on its stars' feet for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:03.600
uncomfortable amounts of time. It is, in the&nbsp;
truest sense, a work of auteur filmmaking.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.040
Yes, that means the movie is uneven and&nbsp;
sometimes flawed, but that lends the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.749
its texture and personality. As Rolling&nbsp;
Stone's Peter Travers wrote in his review,

00:03:19.748 --> 00:03:25.080
However, other outlets, such as the Guardian, felt&nbsp;
that "Once Upon a Time in Hollywood" ceded a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:31.080
too much ground to Tarantino's personal fears and&nbsp;
hang-ups. Critic Wendy Ide noted that some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:35.760
film's choices, like Booth's alleged history of&nbsp;
violence toward women getting mentioned offhand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.980
feel like a jab at the #MeToo movement, while the&nbsp;
story of two performers on a career decline can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:46.860
easily be read as Tarantino's own fear of losing&nbsp;
relevance after so much time in the sun. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.760
the reviewer acknowledges that the movie is&nbsp;
clearly the product of a singular vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.240
writing, "It's a film that could&nbsp;
only have been made by one man."

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.040
Ultimately, "Once Upon a Time in Hollywood"&nbsp;
might be the most "Tarantino" movie yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:04.063
And for the venerated filmmaker himself,&nbsp;
that's an achievement worth celebrating.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:04.868
"We're all good."

00:04:04.868 --> 00:04:05.368
"Don't need to go again?"

00:04:05.368 --> 00:04:07.048
"No, we're done. That was fantastic."

